model.en:
// main.ts
import { useContainer } from 'class-validator';

async function bootstrap() {
  const app = await NestFactory.create(AppModule);
  // Route class-validator constraint instantiation through Nest's DI container
  useContainer(app.select(AppModule), { fallbackOnErrors: true });
  app.useGlobalPipes(new ValidationPipe({ whitelist: true }));
  await app.listen(3000);
}
bootstrap();

Why: by default class-validator does `new IsEmailUniqueConstraint()` itself, outside Nest, so the constructor's UsersRepository is never injected and this.users is undefined. useContainer(app.select(AppModule), ...) tells class-validator to resolve constraints through Nest's container instead, so the @Injectable() constraint gets its repository. fallbackOnErrors: true is required so the built-in validators (@IsEmail, @IsString, …), which are NOT Nest providers, fall back to class-validator's own instantiation instead of throwing 'provider not found'. The constraint class itself needs no change — and it must still be registered as a provider so Nest can build it.

